Nothing kernel OC1 -- unofficial ERNOBUILDS build
================================================

This is an unofficial Nothing kernel modification for Redmi Note 12 Pro 4G
(sweet, Snapdragon 732G / SM7150-AC). It is not an official Glade release.
Credit for Glade belongs to Frnzhermanz and its contributors:

https://github.com/hrihq/Glade-Kernel

Current source and GPLv2 corresponding source
--------------------------------------------

The CPU-OC source baseline is tagged ``nothing-oc1-working`` at:

https://github.com/ERNOBUILDS/Glade-Kernel-unofficial/tree/nothing-oc1-working

The owner reports that OC1 boots and that 2112 MHz little / 2592 MHz big
work and are selectable in Franco Kernel Manager. The tag records the tested
CPU-OC implementation, One UI vmalloc freeze fix, boot-safe PIL copy,
memory-copy/LZ4 changes, diagnostics, packaging source and exact source-form
``arch/arm64/configs/nothing_oc1_defconfig`` configuration. It also records
four pre-existing KernelSU compatibility edits byte-for-byte with the owner's
explicit permission; publication did not edit any KernelSU code.

The kernel is licensed under GPLv2; see COPYING and the retained per-file
licenses/copyrights. This repository provides the corresponding source,
configuration and build/packaging scripts for this from-source OC1 build.
Redistributors must provide the complete matching GPLv2 corresponding source,
not substitute a binary-only patch or rely on source from a different build.
Toolchain/build instructions and the pinned AnyKernel template revision are in
Documentation/nothing-oc1.md and Documentation/nothing-kernel.md. Rebuild byte
identity is not claimed independently of toolchain and build timestamps.
No kernel Images, DT payloads, ZIPs, logs or credentials are committed here;
this source push does not publish release ZIPs.

Experimental overclocking -- use entirely at your own risk
----------------------------------------------------------

CPU overclocking is optional (CONFIG_NOTHING_OC / nothing.oc=0). The targets
are 2.112 GHz little and 2.592 GHz big. The working OC1 voltage/thermal policy
is preserved: the old fixed +32 mV open-loop request is removed; only new LUT
rows use a bounded stock-slope-derived +4--8 mV. Stock voltages and CPR corners
are not raised, and OVERRIDE is not programmed. CPU >=70 C or board/skin proxy
>=42 C caps requests at stock, with 60 C / 39 C resume thresholds. Missing or
stale sensors, suspend and rejected/revoked LUT writes also block OC.

Owner-reported success and software frequency selection are not independent
PLL-lock or actual-rail-voltage measurements. Voltage/thermal margin and
long-term stability are unverified. Risks include boot failure, hangs, reboots,
frame drops, overheating, battery stress, hardware ageing, GPU/CPU instability
and data corruption. No warranty or promise of gaming performance is made.
The installer keeps the "Nothing kernel" name and prints ERNOBUILDS.

Historical pre-OC1 vmalloc binary-patch notice
---------------------------------------------

The following preserved notice concerns the earlier developer-supplied
``4.14.357-glade-g2aba3f843f40-dirty`` binary, NOT this compiled OC1 baseline.
Its unresolved upstream dirty-source gap must not be confused with the
from-source OC1 implementation and configuration described above.

ERNOBUILDS vmalloc lock repair (unofficial)
============================================

Status, attribution, and support
--------------------------------

This branch is an unofficial ERNOBUILDS modification of Glade Kernel. It is
not an official Glade release and is not supported by the Glade developer.
Credit for the original kernel belongs to Glade and its contributors:

https://github.com/hrihq/Glade-Kernel

The branch is based on Glade commit
``2aba3f843f402cc205d5adf3f5d60825b4890e6a``. Its source-level change fixes
the ``mm/vmalloc.c`` sequence iterator: ``s_start()`` acquires
``vmap_purge_lock`` and ``vmap_area_lock``, so ``s_stop()`` now releases both
locks in reverse order.

Binary and source relationship
------------------------------

The tested recovery-flashable kernel was **not compiled from this branch**.
The existing ``4.14.357-glade-g2aba3f843f40-dirty`` ARM64 kernel Image was
patched directly: exactly 24 bytes at decompressed Image offset ``0x139458``
were replaced to add the missing ``mutex_unlock(&vmap_purge_lock)`` call, and
the boot image was then repacked. This branch records the source-equivalent
repair for review and reference; it does not claim byte-for-byte reproduction
of that binary.

Known ``-dirty`` source gap -- placeholder
------------------------------------------

The Glade developer confirmed that the tested ``-dirty`` binary was built
from commit ``2aba3f843f402cc205d5adf3f5d60825b4890e6a`` plus uncommitted local
changes. Those local changes have not yet been provided and are not present in
this branch.

**TODO:** When the developer supplies those changes, preserve and add them
with clear provenance, then document the matching build configuration and
toolchain. Until that happens, this branch must not be described as the exact
or complete corresponding source for the shipped ``-dirty`` binary. Anyone
redistributing that binary under GPLv2 must resolve this source gap rather
than relying on this branch alone.

Linux kernel
============

This file was moved to Documentation/admin-guide/README.rst

Please notice that there are several guides for kernel developers and users.
These guides can be rendered in a number of formats, like HTML and PDF.

In order to build the documentation, use ``make htmldocs`` or
``make pdfdocs``.

There are various text files in the Documentation/ subdirectory,
several of them using the Restructured Text markup notation.
See Documentation/00-INDEX for a list of what is contained in each file.

Please read the Documentation/process/changes.rst file, as it contains the
requirements for building and running the kernel, and information about
the problems which may result by upgrading your kernel.
